Built with [Streamlit](https://streamlit.io/) and [SweatStack](https://sweatstack.no/).

## Setup

### 1. Create a SweatStack Application

Create a new application at [app.sweatstack.no/applications/new](https://app.sweatstack.no/applications/new).

Set the redirect URI to your callback URL:
- Local development: `http://localhost:8080/auth/callback`
- Production: `https://yourdomain.com/auth/callback`

Note down your **Client ID** and **Client Secret**.

### 2. Configure Environment

Copy the environment template and edit it with your credentials:

| macOS / Linux | Windows (PowerShell) |
|---|---|
| `cp .env.template .env` | `Copy-Item .env.template .env` |

Edit `.env`:

```
SWEATSTACK_CLIENT_ID=your_client_id
SWEATSTACK_CLIENT_SECRET=your_client_secret
SECRET_KEY=your_random_secret_key
HTTPS_ONLY=false
APP_URL=http://localhost:8080
```

Generate a secure secret key:

| macOS / Linux | Windows (PowerShell) |
|---|---|
| `openssl rand -base64 32` | `python -c "import secrets; print(secrets.token_urlsafe(32))"` |

### 3. Install Dependencies and Run

```bash
uv sync
docker build -t {{ project_slug }} .
docker run --rm --env-file .env --volume .:/app -p 8080:8080 {{ project_slug }}
```

Open [http://localhost:8080](http://localhost:8080) in your browser.

## Development

The `docker run` command above mounts the current directory, so changes to `streamlit_app.py` will be reflected after a browser refresh.

## Configuration

The proxy (`proxy.py`) is configured entirely with environment variables. Only the first three are required; every other setting has a default that suits most apps.

| Variable | Default | Description |
|----------|---------|-------------|
| `SWEATSTACK_CLIENT_ID` | *required* | OAuth client ID from SweatStack. |
| `SWEATSTACK_CLIENT_SECRET` | *required* | OAuth client secret from SweatStack. |
| `SECRET_KEY` | *required* | Key for encrypting the token cookie. Use a long random string (see above). Changing it logs everyone out. |
| `APP_URL` | `http://localhost:8080` | Public URL of the app. Must match the redirect URI registered with SweatStack (`$APP_URL/auth/callback`). |
| `HTTPS_ONLY` | `true` | Marks cookies `Secure`. Set to `false` only for local development over plain `http://`. |
| `AUTO_LOGIN` | `false` | Send logged-out visitors straight to the SweatStack login instead of showing the app's logged-out page. If a login attempt fails, the user is not sent again for 5 minutes, so a broken login can't loop. |
| `ALLOW_SIGNUP` | `true` | Set to `false` to only let people with an existing SweatStack account log in (sends `email_auth_mode=existing_only` to SweatStack). |
| `FORWARD_REFRESH_TOKEN` | `true` | Also pass the user's refresh token to Streamlit. Read [Tokens in your app](#tokens-in-your-app) before using it. |
| `PWA_INSTALL_BANNER` | `true` | Show logged-in users a banner inviting them to install the app. See [Installable app (PWA)](#installable-app-pwa). |
| `UPSTREAM_URL` | `http://localhost:8501` | Where the proxy reaches Streamlit inside the container. You shouldn't need to change this. |

On/off settings accept `true`/`false`, `1`/`0`, `yes`/`no` or `on`/`off`. An unrecognised value is ignored with a warning in the logs, and the default applies.

## Tokens in Your App

The proxy keeps the user's SweatStack tokens in an encrypted, HTTP-only cookie: the browser stores it, but neither the browser's JavaScript nor your Streamlit code can read it. Instead, the proxy passes the tokens to Streamlit as request headers:

| Header | Contains | When |
|--------|----------|------|
| `X-SweatStack-Token` | Access token (short-lived, about 15 minutes) | The user is logged in |
| `X-SweatStack-Refresh-Token` | Refresh token (long-lived) | The user is logged in and `FORWARD_REFRESH_TOKEN` is not `false` |

`StreamlitAuth.behind_proxy()` reads the access token for you, so most apps never touch these headers. Read them yourself with `st.context.headers`:

```python
refresh_token = st.context.headers.get("X-SweatStack-Refresh-Token")
```

**You can trust these values.** The proxy removes any `X-SweatStack-*` header sent by the browser before adding its own, so these headers only ever contain what the proxy put there. That holds only for traffic that goes through the proxy: never expose Streamlit's own port (8501) publicly. The included `Dockerfile` and `fly.toml` only expose the proxy (port 8080).

### Things to know before using the refresh token

The refresh token is mainly useful for work that happens *outside* a user's session, such as processing a webhook or running a scheduled job for a user who isn't using the app. A typical setup saves it when the user opens the app and uses it later from a background job.

1. **It's a snapshot from when the page was opened.** Streamlit only sees request headers when the browser connects. That happens on page load and on reconnects, not on every interaction. The proxy refreshes tokens in the background, so during a long session the headers your app sees can be out of date. Reloading the page gives fresh ones.

2. **A refresh token can stop working.** The proxy refreshes the user's tokens whenever the access token is about to expire. If SweatStack issues a *new* refresh token in that exchange ("rotation"), the old one may stop working, and the proxy stores the new one in the cookie. A copy you saved earlier can then be invalid. It works the other way too: if a background job uses the refresh token and receives a new one, the copy in the user's cookie may stop working, and the user has to log in again. To stay safe:
   - **Save the token again on every session**: on page load, overwrite the stored copy. The user ID is the `sub` claim of the access token. Don't assume a stored copy stays valid forever.
   - **If the token endpoint returns a new refresh token, save it** and use it from then on.
   - **Expect `invalid_grant` errors.** When a stored token is rejected, mark that user as "needs to log in again" and stop retrying. It's an ordinary event, not an outage.
   - **Don't refresh with it during the session.** Inside the Streamlit session, use the access token (`auth.client`) and leave refreshing to the proxy, so the cookie and your copy can't conflict.

3. **Treat it like a password.** It gives long-term access to the user's data. Never show it in the UI or write it to logs, and remember that `st.write(st.context.headers)` or an error tracker that records request headers would expose it. If you store it, encrypt it at rest.

4. **Not needed? Turn it off.** If your app has no use for the refresh token, set `FORWARD_REFRESH_TOKEN=false` so it never reaches your code.

## Installable App (PWA)

The app can be installed to the home screen (iOS, Android) or as a desktop app (Chrome, Edge). With `manifest.json` in place this works out of the box:

- **Manifest and icons.** `manifest.json` sets the name and colors; the icons live in `static/`.
- **Service worker** (`static/sw.js`, served at `/sw.js`). Chrome requires one before it offers installation. It caches nothing, so every deploy is visible right away; its only other job is to show a short "You're offline" page when a page load fails without a connection.
- **iOS launch screens.** iOS only shows a launch screen if one matches the exact screen size. The proxy generates them on demand for current iPhones and iPads: the 512px icon centred on `background_color`. To use your own design for a size, add `static/splash-<width>x<height>.png` (e.g. `splash-1179x2556.png`); it takes precedence.
- **Install banner** (`static/install-banner.js`). A bar at the bottom of the page, in your `theme_color` (text switches between black and white for contrast), shown to **logged-in users only**:
  - *Chrome, Edge, Samsung Internet (Android and desktop):* "Install *App name*" with an **Install** button that opens the browser's install dialog. It only appears once the browser reports the app as installable and not yet installed.
  - *iPhone and iPad:* instructions to tap Share, then "Add to Home Screen" (iOS has no install button that websites can trigger).
  - *Other browsers, or when the app is already installed:* nothing.

  Closing the banner hides it for 30 days in that browser. For logged-out visitors the browser's own install prompt is suppressed as well, so nobody is asked to install the login page. Turn the banner off with `PWA_INSTALL_BANNER=false`; the app stays installable from the browser menu.

To customise:

1. Edit **`manifest.json`**: set `name`, `short_name`, `theme_color` and `background_color` (use hex colors, e.g. `#ff6600`).
2. Replace the icons in **`static/`** with your own (keep the filenames):
   - `icon-192x192.png`: Android home screen icon
   - `icon-512x512.png`: Android splash screen and generated iOS launch screens
   - `icon-180x180.png`: iOS home screen icon

If `manifest.json` is removed, all PWA features are disabled.

## Customizing Your App

Edit `streamlit_app.py` to build your application. The template provides:

- `auth.is_authenticated()` — Check if user is logged in
- `auth.client` — Authenticated SweatStack API client
- `auth.select_user()` — User selector widget for the sidebar
- `auth.logout_button()` — Logout button widget

Read the documentation for the SweatStack Python library and its Streamlit integration [here](https://docs.sweatstack.no/learn/integrations/streamlit/).

## Production Deployment

For production:

1. Use a strong, unique `SECRET_KEY`
2. Deploy behind HTTPS (required for secure cookies)
3. Update the redirect URI in your SweatStack application settings

### Deploying to Fly.io

Install the [Fly.io CLI](https://fly.io/docs/flyctl/), then:

```bash
fly launch
```

Create a production environment file:

| macOS / Linux | Windows (PowerShell) |
|---|---|
| `cp .env.template .env.production` | `Copy-Item .env.template .env.production` |

Configure it with your production `APP_URL` (e.g. `https://your-app.fly.dev`), then import secrets:

| macOS / Linux | Windows (PowerShell) |
|---|---|
| `cat .env.production \| fly secrets import` | `Get-Content .env.production \| fly secrets import` |

Redeploy with `fly deploy` when you make changes.

## Updating the Template

This project was generated with [Copier](https://copier.readthedocs.io/). To pull in template updates:

```bash
copier update
```

Copier performs a three-way merge, so your customisations are preserved. Only lines changed by both you and the template will produce conflicts.

Before updating, read the [changelog](https://github.com/SweatStack/sweatstack-streamlit-template/blob/main/CHANGELOG.md). Its *Updating* section has step-by-step instructions (written so you can also hand them to a coding agent), including for projects created before the template used Copier.

